"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][21]={"school_physics_en:M21-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M21-L1 – Photon Energy and the External Photoelectric Effect<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Quantum objects and photoelectric effect<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why might intense red light fail to eject electrons from a particular\nmetal, while weaker violet light succeeds?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Electromagnetic radiation exchanges energy in individual quanta. A\n<strong>photon<\/strong> of frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncarries energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{ph}=hf=hc/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.62607015<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>34<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=6.62607015\\times10^{-34}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis Planck’s constant (defined exactly) and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>299<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>792<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>458<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=299\\,792\\,458\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the speed of light in vacuum. The conversion is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.602176634<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{eV}=1.602176634\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHigher frequency means more energy <strong>per photon<\/strong>; greater\nintensity at fixed frequency means more photons per unit time and\narea.<\/p>\n<p>In the <strong>external photoelectric effect<\/strong>, a material\nsurface absorbs a photon and may emit an electron. The photon must\nsupply at least the <strong>work function<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>W<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof that material and surface. In the simplified model, the maximum\nkinetic energy obeys Einstein’s equation:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">when <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{\\mathrm{max}}=hf-W\\quad\\text{when }hf\\ge W.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Below the <strong>threshold frequency<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>W<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_0=W/h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nno photoelectrons appear, regardless of intensity in the usual\none-photon regime. Above threshold, greater intensity can increase the\nnumber of emitted electrons but not their maximum kinetic energy. With\nthe <strong>stopping-potential method<\/strong>, the magnitude of the\nstopping voltage\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{\\mathrm{max}}=eV_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe positive elementary-charge magnitude. A graph of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas slope\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h/e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis provides evidence for photons; the national KMK core specifies\nrelationships among energy, momentum, frequency and wavelength rather\nthan requiring one particular photoelectric experimental setup.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_126_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Energy bars for photon energy, work function and maximum kinetic energy beside the frequency threshold.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Energy bars for photon energy, work\nfunction and maximum kinetic energy beside the frequency\nthreshold.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In the one-photon model photoemission requires light above a\nthreshold frequency.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>400<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">400\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nphoton has approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mn>400<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{ph}=1240\\,\\mathrm{eV\\,nm}/400\\,\\mathrm{nm}=3.10\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the surface work function is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=2.20\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe maximum electron kinetic energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.90\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncorresponding to a stopping-voltage magnitude of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.90\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>620<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">620\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe photon has only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.00\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso this idealised one-photon model predicts no emission.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Use a photoelectric-effect simulation. Keep frequency above threshold\nand change intensity. Then hold intensity constant and change frequency.\nRecord photocurrent and stopping voltage separately. Avoid unshielded\nultraviolet sources and exposed high voltages.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to the energy of a photon when its frequency\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{ph}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin eV for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find maximum kinetic energy for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{ph}=3.20\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=2.50\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why cannot high intensity replace a threshold frequency in this\nmodel?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It doubles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1240<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>500<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.48<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_\\mathrm{ph}\\approx1240/500=2.48\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{\\mathrm{max}}=0.70\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.70<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V_s=0.70\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Each single photon must supply enough energy to overcome the work\nfunction.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>This relation applies in the ordinary linear one-photon regime and\nuses a simplified surface model. Real surfaces, contact potentials and\nother material effects may affect measurements. Multiphoton effects from\nextremely intense lasers are excluded.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L7<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L2<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.3: quantum\nobjects; common and additional advanced topics · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/6-2-photoelectric-effect\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §6.2 Photoelectric Effect<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §6.2: threshold\nfrequency, energy and stopping potential · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/quantenphysik/quantenobjekt-photon/grundwissen/einsteins-theorie-des-lichts\">LEIFIphysik\n– Einstein’s photon model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: photon energy, momentum and\nEinstein equation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M21-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M21-L2 – Extension: Compton Scattering and Photon Momentum<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Quantum objects and photoelectric effect<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A scattered X-ray photon leaves a collision with a slightly longer\nwavelength. Where did some of its initial energy go?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Extension/example, not an explicitly named mandatory national\ncore topic:<\/strong> Besides energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=hf<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nphotons carry directed <strong>momentum<\/strong> with magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=E/c=h/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPhotons can transfer momentum to matter despite having no rest mass. In\n<strong>Compton scattering<\/strong>, a high-energy photon interacts with\nan electron; after scattering, the photon may have a longer wavelength\nand less energy. Total energy and vector momentum are conserved; some\nenergy becomes electron kinetic energy.<\/p>\n<p>For an initially approximately free electron at rest, relativistic\nconservation leads to<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda=\\lambda&#39;-\\lambda=\\frac{h}{m_ec}(1-\\cos\\theta).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the photon’s scattering angle and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe electron mass. The quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.426<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h/(m_ec)=2.426\\,\\mathrm{pm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the <strong>electron Compton wavelength<\/strong>. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe shift is zero; at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.426<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.426\\,\\mathrm{pm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>180<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">180^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is twice this value. The result highlights why an energy balance\nalone is not enough: momentum is a vector, so scattering direction\nmatters. Compton scattering is a useful supplement illustrating the KMK\ncore relationships between energy, momentum and wavelength; the named\nexperiment itself is not compulsory in the national table.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_127_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Incoming photon, scattered photon and recoiling electron form a schematic momentum picture.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Incoming photon, scattered photon and\nrecoiling electron form a schematic momentum picture.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Compton scattering transfers photon energy and vector momentum to\nan electron.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An incident photon has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>70.000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=70.000\\,\\mathrm{pm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt a scattering angle of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.426<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda=2.426\\,\\mathrm{pm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>72.426<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&#39;=72.426\\,\\mathrm{pm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe outgoing photon’s momentum is smaller. One cannot simply subtract\nthe scalar magnitudes to obtain the recoil electron’s momentum; the\nvector directions matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>On paper draw the incident photon momentum to the right, the\nscattered photon momentum upward through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>90<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the resulting recoil electron momentum. Use a mathematical model\nonly; no X-ray source.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.626<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>34<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=6.626\\times10^{-34}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=0^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>180<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=180^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the Compton effect more than a change in the number of\nphotons?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.33<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>27<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=h/\\lambda\\approx1.33\\times10^{-27}\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2.426<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.852<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda=2\\times2.426=4.852\\,\\mathrm{pm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The wavelength, hence the energy and momentum of individual photons,\nchanges depending on angle.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The formula assumes a free electron initially at rest. Binding\neffects, scattering from other particles and multiple scattering are\nexcluded. The vector sketch is schematic, not to scale.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L1<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L3<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.3: quantum\nobjects; common and additional advanced topics · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/6-3-the-compton-effect\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §6.3 The Compton Effect<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §6.3: Compton\nshift and relativistic scattering · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M21-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M21-L3 – De Broglie Wavelength and Matter Waves<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Quantum objects and photoelectric effect<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why can an electron beam produce a diffraction pattern from crystals\neven though electrons have rest mass?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>de Broglie hypothesis<\/strong> assigns a wavelength to a\nfree particle with momentum magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>h<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\mathrm{dB}=\\frac{h}{p}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a slow particle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx mv<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn electron accelerated from rest through a potential difference of\nmagnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngains kinetic energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=eU=p^2/(2m_e)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the ideal nonrelativistic approximation. Therefore<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>h<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\mathrm{dB}=\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2m_e eU}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This wavelength is not the physical diameter of the electron, nor\ndoes it imply that a material string is waving. The quantum description\npredicts observable diffraction and interference probabilities. A larger\nmomentum gives a shorter wavelength. Electron diffraction by crystals\nsupports the model because atomic lattice spacings can be comparable to\nthe electron wavelength. For an ordinary macroscopic object,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h/p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis far too small to reveal interference in everyday conditions. At\nsufficiently high accelerating voltage one must replace the\nnonrelativistic momentum by its relativistic expression. The de Broglie\nrelation belongs to the KMK core relationships used to describe quantum\nobjects.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_128_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Decreasing electron wavelength model curve labelled at 150 and 600 volts.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Decreasing electron wavelength model\ncurve labelled at 150 and 600 volts.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Electron matter wavelength decreases with the square root of\nvoltage in the nonrelativistic approximation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An electron is accelerated from rest by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=150\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.109<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>31<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_e=9.109\\times10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.602<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>19<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\mathrm{dB}=h/\\sqrt{2m_e eU}\\approx1.00\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm m=0.100\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe wavelength is half as large because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\propto U^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Make a table of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=150<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>300<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRelate these scales to atomic lattice spacing and use a simulation\ninstead of an electron-beam apparatus.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What goes in the denominator of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=h/p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the electron wavelength at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.100\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">150\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does an ordinary ball not show obvious de Broglie\ninterference?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is misleading about describing an electron as a vibrating\nmaterial thread?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Momentum magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>4<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.050<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.100/\\sqrt4=0.050\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its momentum is large enough to make the wavelength extremely small;\npractical interference conditions are unavailable.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The matter wave is a quantum description, not a classical material\nstring.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The formula assumes acceleration from rest, a simple potential\ndifference, negligible losses and nonrelativistic speeds. Real electron\nscattering depends on crystal structure and interactions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L2<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L4<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.3: quantum\nobjects; common and additional advanced topics · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/quantenphysik/quantenobjekt-elektron/grundwissen/de-broglie-wellenlaenge\">LEIFIphysik\n– de Broglie wavelength<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: λ = h/p and electrons · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/6-5-de-broglies-matter-waves\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §6.5 De Broglie’s Matter Waves<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §6.5:\nmatter waves and momentum · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M21-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M21-L4 – The Double Slit with Individual Quantum Objects<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Quantum objects and photoelectric effect<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Electrons hit a detector as isolated points. Why does a stripe-like\npattern emerge after many repetitions?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>In a <strong>low-intensity double-slit experiment<\/strong>, the\nsource is adjusted so that, ideally, just one electron or photon\noccupies the apparatus at a time. Each screen detection is a single,\nspatially localised event. After many identically prepared runs, a\nreproducible <strong>frequency distribution<\/strong> with bright and\ndark regions appears. The next individual hit cannot be predicted\nexactly; the probability of a hit within a range can be calculated.<\/p>\n<p>When no distinguishable path information exists, both alternatives\ncontribute coherently to the interference pattern. Mathematically one\nadds <strong>probability amplitudes<\/strong>, not the separate\nprobabilities first. In the idealised arrangement the detection density\nis proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi_1+\\psi_2|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot generally\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi_1|^2+|\\psi_2|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe cross term produces interference. With many events the observed\nhistogram approaches the predicted distribution. A point-like hit is not\na photograph of a definite classical trajectory through a particular\nslit. Nor does an electron form two ordinary classical pieces of charged\nmatter that later reconnect. If a demonstration uses synthetic events,\nthose data must be identified as simulated rather than measured.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_129_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A few isolated points compared with many simulated events clustered into stripes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A few isolated points compared with many\nsimulated events clustered into stripes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The comparison shows how a statistical pattern emerges from\nindividual simulated detections.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Suppose a toy detector model assigns probabilities\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A)=0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B)=0.30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(C)=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto three regions. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent, identically prepared events, the <strong>expected<\/strong>\ncounts are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>100<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>60<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>40<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nActual observed counts will fluctuate. Expected counts are not\nguaranteed measurement results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Simulate 200 individual detections and build a histogram. Clearly\nlabel the events as simulated. Compare a single dot, twenty events and\nthe full distribution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What can be predicted with certainty about the next individual\nscreen position?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does a point-like detection differ from an interference\ndistribution?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What expected count corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0.30<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why are separate single-slit probabilities insufficient when two\ncoherent alternatives are available?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The exact position cannot generally be predicted; only probabilities\ncan be assigned.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">One point is one event; the distribution is built from many\nrepetitions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\cdot P=60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexpected events.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The amplitudes interfere; the cross terms generally do not\nvanish.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The numerical probabilities in the example are invented teaching\nvalues for a counting model, not measured double-slit data. Real\npatterns depend on preparation, aperture widths, coherence and detection\ngeometry.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L3<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L5<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.3: quantum\nobjects; common and additional advanced topics · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/quantenphysik/quantenobjekt-elektron/versuche/simulationen-zum-doppelspalt\">LEIFIphysik\n– Double-slit simulations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Experiments: individual events,\ninterference and path information · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/quantenphysik/wesenszuege-quantenphysik/grundwissen/wesenszug-2-faehigkeit-zur-interferenz\">LEIFIphysik\n– Ability to interfere<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: single events and statistical\npattern · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M21-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M21-L5 – Probability, Superposition and Complementarity<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Quantum objects and photoelectric effect<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does interference disappear when a detector reliably identifies\nthe slit traversed by a quantum object?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Superposition<\/strong> means that, when a quantum object has\ncoherently prepared alternatives, its state is described by their\ncombined probability amplitudes. For two paths we may write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi=\\psi_1+\\psi_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe probability density is then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Re<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi|^2=|\\psi_1|^2+|\\psi_2|^2+2\\operatorname{Re}(\\psi_1^*\\psi_2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe last quantity is the <strong>interference term<\/strong>: it may be\npositive or negative. Interference is not just the arithmetic sum of two\nindependent detection intensities.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Complementarity of path information and interference<\/strong>\nmeans that, when path alternatives are reliably physically\ndistinguishable, the screen pattern obtained without conditioning on the\npath loses the coherent cross terms. Nobody must consciously look at a\nresult. The relevant process is interaction or entanglement with a path\ndetector or environment. If paths remain indistinguishable, interference\nvisibility can be high. Partial distinguishability may leave reduced\nfringes. An approximate diagnostic is fringe visibility\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=(I_{\\max}-I_{\\min})/(I_{\\max}+I_{\\min})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA quantum experiment can thus have a <strong>predictable statistical\ndistribution<\/strong> even though individual outcomes are stochastic.\nThese are compatible statements.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_130_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two schematic detection profiles with indistinguishable and distinguishable paths.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two schematic detection profiles with\nindistinguishable and distinguishable paths.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Physically available which-path information suppresses\nunconditioned interference.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>In a model intensity profile, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\max}=90<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\min}=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts per interval. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>90<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=(90-10)/(90+10)=0.80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf coupling to a path marker yields equal maxima and minima of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>50<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach, the visibility is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese numbers are <strong>illustrative<\/strong>, not real experimental\nmeasurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Prepare two schematic experimental conditions on paper: no path\ndetector and physically distinguishable paths. State which information\nis available in each case and which histogram is expected. Do not invoke\nhuman awareness as the mechanism.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What quantities are added in coherent quantum superposition?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>V<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\max}=80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{\\min}=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is it sufficient for a person to consciously watch the apparatus to\nerase fringes?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can partially distinguishable paths cause partly reduced\nvisibility?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Probability amplitudes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=(80-20)/(80+20)=0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. A physical interaction making path information available is\nrelevant.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes. Visibility can lie between zero and one.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The visibility expression assumes properly defined maximum and\nminimum in one comparable intensity profile. Real path-marker\ninteractions require a more complete quantum treatment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L4<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L6<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.3: quantum\nobjects; common and additional advanced topics · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/quantenphysik/wesenszuege-quantenphysik/grundwissen/wesenszug-4-komplementaritaet\">LEIFIphysik\n– Complementarity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: distinguishable paths and loss of\ninterference · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/quantenphysik/wesenszuege-quantenphysik/grundwissen/wesenszug-2-faehigkeit-zur-interferenz\">LEIFIphysik\n– Ability to interfere<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: single events and statistical\npattern · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M21-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M21-L6 – Quantum Models and the Limits of Classical Pictures<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Quantum objects and photoelectric effect<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Does a single dot on a detection screen prove that the electron\nfollowed a definite classical path beforehand?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Classical models<\/strong> work well when continuously defined\nquantities under appropriate conditions describe predictable\ntrajectories. In the double-slit experiment, however, simple trajectory\npictures do not jointly explain localised single events and the\nstatistical interference distribution when path information is\nunavailable. A <strong>quantum model<\/strong> assigns a state to a\nspecified preparation and predicts probabilities for possible\nmeasurement outcomes. The <strong>distribution<\/strong> can be\npredictable with high precision while a single detection position\nremains stochastic.<\/p>\n<p>Four terms need distinction: <strong>reality<\/strong> concerns the\nrelation between physical states and observations;\n<strong>locality<\/strong> concerns influences or correlations between\nspatially separated systems; <strong>causality<\/strong> concerns\ncause–effect explanations and models; and <strong>determinism<\/strong>\nasks whether a state uniquely fixes individual future outcomes. Quantum\nexperiments constrain naive classical assumptions but do not\nautomatically select a unique philosophical interpretation. In\nparticular, the inability to predict single hits does\n<strong>not<\/strong> mean that experiments are arbitrary or that physics\nlacks causal structure. Probabilities depend on reproducible preparation\nand apparatus geometry.<\/p>\n<p>Saying “the electron is literally two tiny balls at once”\nmisrepresents superposition. Nor does a single hit retrospectively\ncertify an unobserved classical path. Scientific models are assessed by\npredictive power, testability and limits of validity, not only by how\neasy they are to visualise.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_131_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three adjacent panels show one detection dot, a probability curve and an interpretation question.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three adjacent panels show one detection\ndot, a probability curve and an interpretation question.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>An observed event, a predictive model and a philosophical\ninterpretation are distinct levels.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Assume a model predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0.40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor one detector region. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=500<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nidentically prepared events, the expected count is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">NP=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nObserving 194 or 209 hits does not by itself falsify the model:\nstatistical fluctuations are predicted. For independent binomial trials\nthe expected standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>120<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=\\sqrt{NP(1-P)}=\\sqrt{120}\\approx11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Critically discuss three statements: “A screen dot shows a path”,\n“Individual events are unpredictable but distributions are not”, and\n“Only conscious observation produces measurement outcomes”. Classify\neach as observation, model claim or unsupported interpretation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a single detector dot not reconstruct a unique\ntrajectory?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0.40<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=500<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfind the expected count.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do stochastic individual outcomes differ from arbitrary model\nparameters?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does observed interference select only one interpretation of quantum\nmechanics?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It gives one detection location, not the unobserved path\nbeforehand.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">200 expected hits.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Outcomes may be stochastic while distributions are determined by the\nspecified preparation and experiment.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Several interpretations share experimentally confirmed\npredictions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Worldview concepts are subtle and interpretation-dependent. One\ndouble-slit experiment does not establish broad claims about every form\nof locality or causality. Quantitative predictions apply only to\nspecified preparations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L5<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L7<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.3: quantum\nobjects; common and additional advanced topics · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/quantenphysik/wesenszuege-quantenphysik/grundwissen/quantenobjekte\">LEIFIphysik\n– Quantum objects<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: statistics and quantum-object concepts ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/quantenphysik/wesenszuege-quantenphysik/grundwissen/wesenszug-4-komplementaritaet\">LEIFIphysik\n– Complementarity<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: distinguishable paths and loss of\ninterference · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M21-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M21-L7 – Advanced Level: Wave Functions and Position–Momentum Uncertainty<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M21 – Quantum objects and photoelectric effect<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a sharply localised electron wave packet require a broader\nspread of possible momentum values?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Additional advanced-level content (eA):<\/strong> The\n<strong>wave function<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(x,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis generally complex-valued. Its squared magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi(x,t)|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents a <strong>probability density<\/strong> for position in one\ndimension. The probability for an interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[a,b]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(a\\le x\\le b)=\\int_a^b |\\psi(x,t)|^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a normalised state the integral over all space is one. A high\ncurve value at one point is <strong>not<\/strong> a probability exceeding\none: density has units, and the integrated area over an interval\nrepresents probability. The <strong>position–momentum uncertainty\nrelation<\/strong> for standard deviations on the same axis is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mfrac><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x\\,\\Delta p_x\\ge\\frac{\\hbar}{2},\\qquad \\hbar=\\frac{h}{2\\pi}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It gives a fundamental limit on how sharply a quantum state can\nspecify position and momentum together, not merely how good the\ninstruments are. A strongly localised packet requires many momentum\ncomponents; a nearly pure momentum state spreads broadly in space. An\nimportant precision: this inequality concerns the <strong>standard\ndeviations<\/strong> of the respective observables, not arbitrary\nmeasurement-error bars. It does not replace a full dynamical\ncalculation. Both the qualitative wave-function interpretation and the\nposition–momentum relation are identified by the KMK as additional\nadvanced-level topics.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_132_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two states with inversely related position and momentum spreads.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two states with inversely related\nposition and momentum spreads.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A more localised wave packet must have a broader momentum\ndistribution.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For an electron with position standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=1.0\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.055<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>34<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar\\approx1.055\\times10^{-34}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.27<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>26<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p_x\\ge\\hbar/(2\\Delta x)=5.27\\times10^{-26}\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe actual momentum spread may be larger. For a normalised illustrative\nbox model with constant density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi|^2=0.20\\,\\mathrm{nm}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover a length of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5.0\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntotal probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.20\\times5.0=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw two normalised schematic position densities: one narrow and one\nbroad. Sketch the associated momentum distributions qualitatively. Use\nwave-packet and Fourier reasoning, not a presumed classical particle\npath.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi(x,t)|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmean in one spatial dimension?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the lower bound for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=2.0\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a large probability-density value not itself a\nprobability?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot automatically identical to a ruler’s measurement error?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Probability density per unit length.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>2.64<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>26<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p_x\\ge2.64\\times10^{-26}\\,\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Probabilities are integrals over intervals; the density itself may\nexceed one.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is a state-related standard deviation, not simply instrumental\nimprecision.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The uniform-box example illustrates normalisation only, not a\ncomplete solution of a physical potential. The given uncertainty\ninequality uses standard deviations; different uncertainty measures\nrequire separate mathematical results.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite <strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L1 – Historical Atomic\nModels: Observations and Explanations<\/a>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.3: quantum\nobjects; common and additional advanced topics · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/7-1-wave-functions\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §7.1 Wave Functions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §7.1: normalisation\nand Born probability interpretation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/7-2-the-heisenberg-uncertainty-principle\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §7.2 Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\n§7.2: position–momentum uncertainty · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
